th("Let me spend a minute on wax, because this is the choice that overwhelms beginners the most, and it really does not need to.")
th("You will see soy, paraffin, coconut, beeswax, and a dozen blends, and every supplier swears theirs is the best. For your first candles, ignore almost all of it.")
br("Several different bags of wax lined up on the bench, one pulled forward.","lines up a few different bags of wax on the bench and pulls one forward, setting it apart from the rest")
th("Start with a single soy container wax. It is forgiving, it is widely available, it cleans up with soap and water, and it is made for exactly what you are doing.")
th("Soy is more patient with temperature mistakes than most waxes, which matters enormously when you are still learning to read your thermometer.")
th("Once you have made a dozen good soy candles, then by all means go explore coconut blends and the fancy stuff. But learn to walk on soy first.")
th("Picking one wax and staying with it for your first month teaches you more than hopping between five waxes ever could, because you learn how that one behaves.")
# ===== ACT 7c reading a fragrance label =====
th("Now fragrance, because this is where beginners spend the most and waste the most. Let me save you some money and some heartache.")
th("Every fragrance oil has a maximum load, usually printed on the bottle, often around ten percent. That is the most the wax can actually hold.")
br("An amber fragrance bottle being turned to show the load percentage on its label.","turns an amber fragrance bottle to face the camera, pointing at the load percentage on the label")
th("Beginners assume more oil means more scent, so they pour in extra, and the wax simply cannot absorb it. It pools, it ruins the burn, and it can even be unsafe.")
th("Start at around six to eight percent by weight, measured on a scale, not by eye. A little less fragrance that actually binds beats a lot that just sits there.")
th("And give your fragrance a curing period. A soy candle that has rested for a week or two will throw far more scent than one you burn the same day you pour it.")
th("So buy one fragrance you genuinely love, measure it properly, and let it cure. That single habit will make your candles smell better than most beginners ever manage.")
